{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/scale-demo-overreach-review.md", "content": "# Avoiding overreach from sparse scaling evidence

A demo in which a 1x1 input becomes a 2x2 output is consistent with several rules: global doubling, local cell expansion, fixed minimum output size, or a task-specific behavior. It does not establish how each cell of a heterogeneous larger grid maps or whether its dimensions should double. Before predicting a large test from that clue, obtain a structurally informative example and compare the complete output against candidate mechanisms. After a failed scaled submission, treat the failure as rejecting that output—not as evidence for a particular alternative. Rebuild the hypothesis from all pairs and request a demo only for a distinction that could change the prediction.

In the reviewed instance, the 1x1→2x2 pair was insufficient to justify naively duplicating each test cell into a 2x2 block; the later 10x10→20x20 pair showed output shape doubling but did not by itself give evidence for applying that cell replication to the separate 4x4 test. Keep output-size clues distinct from the transformation rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u000/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/scale-demo-overreach-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/session-18868900-output-shape-review.md", "content": "# Session review: periodic lattice to compact output

This session is a cautionary example of under-validated transfer. Demos supplied repetitive stripe/lattice grids with compact outputs of several different dimensions (5×6, 2×4, 5×4, 3×2), while the test had a 28×27 periodic lattice. A small guessed grid based on salient marker cells was submitted without a demonstrated derivation of its dimensions or cell mapping; it was incorrect. A second answer likewise used an unvalidated extraction rule and was incorrect.

Reusable lesson: before transferring a periodic or lattice transformation, explicitly derive both (a) output dimensions and (b) the mapping from complete input units/coordinates to output cells, using every demo. Do not confuse a visually plausible marker subset with the actual extraction rule. After binary rejection, it is not enough to alter one edge or color: reassess shape and mapping together, and request a demo only when a specific competing mapping can be distinguished.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u000/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/session-18868900-output-shape-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-rejection-recovery", "file": "references/repeated-fallback-loop.md", "content": "# Repeated fallback submissions after rejection

An incorrect submission rejects the complete grid and gives no localization. Repeating the same blank/background-only candidate, or making cosmetic edits to it, cannot add information and wastes attempt budget. Before retrying, identify a materially changed hypothesis and check it against every complete demo, including output shape, role colors, changed-cell mask, and preservation. If no revised rule is supported, request a demonstration only when a concrete observation could distinguish live alternatives and change the test prediction. With heterogeneous demos, compare functional roles and changed masks; do not let one visually salient example override the rest.
